  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "Trigonometric Fourier Series Form",
    "lesson_core_move": "Express a periodic continuous-time signal in trigonometric Fourier series form by organizing its DC term and sine and cosine harmonics at integer multiples of the fundamental angular frequency.",
    "section_count": 4,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Trigonometric Fourier Series Form",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Trigonometric Fourier Series Form",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A periodic continuous-time signal is written as a DC component plus sinusoidal harmonics at integer multiples of a fundamental angular frequency $\\omega_0$. In this form, the template tells you where the constant level goes, where the cosine terms go, and where the sine terms go.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\frac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\left[a_k\\cos(k\\omega_0 t)+b_k\\sin(k\\omega_0 t)\\right]",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the template, $\\frac{a_0}{2}$ is the DC term, $a_k$ multiplies the cosine harmonic $\\cos(k\\omega_0 t)$, and $b_k$ multiplies the sine harmonic $\\sin(k\\omega_0 t)$. The index $k$ labels the harmonics, so each positive integer $k$ gives one cosine term and one sine term at frequency $k\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\frac{a_0}{2}+a_1\\cos(\\omega_0 t)+b_1\\sin(\\omega_0 t)+\\cdots",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the simplest base case, read the first harmonic as $a_1\\cos(\\omega_0 t)+b_1\\sin(\\omega_0 t)$ added to the DC term $\\frac{a_0}{2}$. The next steps will fill this same template term by term, using the same notation and the same harmonic labels.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The key recognition cue is simple: when you see a periodic continuous-time signal written in trigonometric Fourier series form, look for the DC term first, then the cosine and sine harmonic pairs at $k\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Trigonometric Fourier Series Form",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Writing the DC Term and First Harmonic Terms",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Write the DC term and first harmonic terms of the trigonometric Fourier series for a signal with $a_0=6$, $a_1=4$, and $b_1=-2$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the template from the previous step. The DC term comes from $a_0/2$, and the first harmonic uses $a_1\\cos(\\omega_0 t)$ and $b_1\\sin(\\omega_0 t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\frac{a_0}{2}+a_1\\cos(\\omega_0 t)+b_1\\sin(\\omega_0 t)=\\frac{6}{2}+4\\cos(\\omega_0 t)+(-2)\\sin(\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The constant term is $\\frac{a_0}{2}=\\frac{6}{2}=3$. The first harmonic is $4\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-2\\sin(\\omega_0 t)$, with the minus sign staying attached to the sine coefficient.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=3+4\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-2\\sin(\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the filled-in partial trigonometric Fourier series is $x(t)=3+4\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-2\\sin(\\omega_0 t)$. This is the first concrete fill-in after the template, with only the constant term and the $k=1$ pair used.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Trigonometric Fourier Series Form",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Writing Sine and Cosine Terms at Higher Harmonic Frequencies",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Write the second- and third-harmonic terms of the trigonometric Fourier series for a signal with coefficients $a_2=-1$, $b_2=5$, $a_3=2$, and $b_3=-4$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that this step asks for higher-harmonic terms, so each coefficient pair stays attached to its own harmonic index. Use $k=2$ with $2\\omega_0$ and $k=3$ with $3\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(-1)\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)+5\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)+2\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)+(-4)\\sin(3\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now keep the signs attached to the coefficients and simplify each term. The second-harmonic pair is $-\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)+5\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)$, and the third-harmonic pair is $2\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)-4\\sin(3\\omega_0 t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "-\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)+5\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)+2\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)-4\\sin(3\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the higher-harmonic contribution is $-\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)+5\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)+2\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)-4\\sin(3\\omega_0 t)$. The rule is the same as before: keep each cosine-sine pair with the same $k$ and only change the frequency label to $k\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Trigonometric Fourier Series Form",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Writing the General Trigonometric Fourier Series Sum",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Write the full trigonometric Fourier series for a periodic signal with DC term $a_0/2$ and harmonic coefficients $a_1,b_1,a_2,b_2,a_3,b_3$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that this step is asking for the compact full series, so keep the DC term outside the harmonic sum and keep each cosine-sine pair together under the same harmonic index $k$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\frac{a_0}{2}+a_1\\cos(\\omega_0 t)+b_1\\sin(\\omega_0 t)+a_2\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)+b_2\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)+a_3\\cos(3\\omega_0 t)+b_3\\sin(3\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now rewrite the repeated harmonic pattern with a summation. The index $k=1,2,3,\\dots$ runs over all positive harmonics, and each value of $k$ contributes one cosine term and one sine term at frequency $k\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\frac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\left[a_k\\cos(k\\omega_0 t)+b_k\\sin(k\\omega_0 t)\\right]",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the compact full trigonometric Fourier series is $x(t)=\\frac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\left[a_k\\cos(k\\omega_0 t)+b_k\\sin(k\\omega_0 t)\\right]$. The explicit harmonic terms and the summation form describe the same structure, with the DC term outside and the harmonic pair inside each $k$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
